<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/foundational/energy-conservation">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Physics. You need nothing from earlier lessons — only everyday
    arithmetic (multiply and divide) and a willingness to think slowly. Everything else is
    built here, step by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain what physicists mean by energy, and why its conservation is the most trusted law in science;</li>
    <li>use the two formulas behind every moving or lifted object, and say what each symbol means;</li>
    <li>calculate work, power, density and pressure — and say what each one physically measures;</li>
    <li>predict whether something floats or sinks, and justify the prediction;</li>
    <li>read an electricity bill or a power-station efficiency figure and know exactly what the numbers are counting.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever a formula appears, we ask what it means before how to compute it. The
    computing is easy; the meaning is the physics.
  </p>

  <h2>The puzzle that starts physics: where does motion go?</h2>
  <p>
    Throw a ball straight up and watch it. It leaves your hand fast, slows, stops for one
    instant at the top, and falls back into your hand — arriving almost as fast as it left.
    Something was spent on the way up and handed back on the way down. Nothing visible was
    stored anywhere. So what was going on?
  </p>
  <p>
    Physics answers with the single most trusted idea in science: <strong>energy</strong>.
    Energy is the universe's accounting system. Every process — a cart rolling down a hill, a
    battery draining, your breakfast being digested — transfers energy from one store to
    another, and the books always balance. Energy cannot be created or destroyed, only moved
    between stores or spread out. That is the <strong>conservation of energy</strong>, and it is
    trusted precisely because every attempt to catch it cheating — from Victorian
    perpetual-motion machines to modern cold-fusion claims — has failed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the motion that disappears at the top of a throw is not vanishing
    — it is energy changing from one form to another and back again. Now we name the forms and
    learn to count them.
  </p>

  <h2>The two stores that run the moving world</h2>
  <p>
    A moving object carries <strong>kinetic energy</strong> — the energy of motion. Its size is
    fixed by one formula: <code>KE = ½·m·v²</code>. Read the symbols: <em>m</em> is the mass in
    kilograms, <em>v</em> the speed in metres per second. The square matters: double a cart's
    speed and its kinetic energy does not double — it quadruples. That little superscript 2 is
    why high-speed crashes are so much worse than low-speed ones.
  </p>
  <p>
    An elevated object carries <strong>gravitational potential energy</strong> — energy stored
    by being lifted against gravity: <code>GPE = m·g·h</code>. Here <em>g</em> is 9.8 newtons
    per kilogram, the strength of gravity near Earth's surface, and <em>h</em> is the height in
    metres. Lift the same object twice as high and the stored energy exactly doubles — the
    formula is honest and linear in height.
  </p>
  <p>
    On an ideal, frictionless track the two stores trade back and forth, and their sum never
    changes. The cart below starts at height h₀ from rest, so its speed at any point is fixed
    by how much height it has lost.
  </p>
          
  <Widget no="1" caption="two hills, one budget — lower the start height and watch the cart fail the first hill" widget="plotter" config={coasterCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> set the start height h₀ to about 6 metres and the red
    speed curve snaps to zero where the blue track rises above the start height. That break is
    the conservation law speaking — no energy, no climb. Roller-coaster designers live inside
    this diagram, which is why the first hill is always the tallest and every later hill must
    be shorter: friction leaks a little of the budget with every metre of track, and there is
    no overdraft facility.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> motion and height are two banks of the same account. Kinetic
    energy (½·m·v²) and gravitational potential energy (m·g·h) convert into each other, and the
    total stays fixed unless something leaks it away.
  </p>

  <h2>The stores, named: every form energy takes</h2>
  <p>
    Kinetic and gravitational are just two of physics's named <strong>stores</strong>. There is
    elastic energy (a stretched spring, a bent bow), chemical energy (food, fuel, batteries),
    nuclear energy (locked in nuclei), and thermal energy (the random jiggling of particles —
    what we feel as heat). Energy moves between stores in four ways: mechanical working (a
    force pushing), electrical working (currents), heating, and radiation (light).
  </p>
  <p>
    A burning match moves energy from the chemical store into thermal and light. Naming the
    stores keeps the accounting honest — and it exposes a crucial fact: <strong>energy is a
    scalar</strong>. Unlike force, energy has no direction. You add up the stores with plain
    arithmetic, which is why conservation is such a clean law to use.
  </p>
  <p>
    The unit is the <strong>joule</strong> (J) — lifting an apple (about 100 grams) one metre
    takes roughly 1 J. Benchmarks to calibrate the number: a full smartphone charge ≈ 10,000 J;
    a chocolate bar ≈ 1,000,000 J; a lightning bolt ≈ 1,000,000,000 J.
  </p>

  <h2>Work: paying for energy with force × distance</h2>
  <p>
    When energy is transferred by a force acting along a distance, physicists call the payment
    <strong>work</strong>: <code>W = F·d</code> — force times the distance moved <em>in the
    direction of the force</em>. Its unit is the joule (1 J = 1 newton-metre). Worked example,
    from the ground up: lifting a 2 kg box 1.5 m at steady speed needs an upward force equal to
    the box's weight, 2 × 9.8 = 19.6 N. The work done is 19.6 × 1.5 ≈ 29 J — and sure enough,
    the box has gained 29 J of gravitational potential energy (m·g·h again).
  </p>
  <p>
    The clause "in the direction of the force" is where marks are won and lost. Carrying the
    box <em>horizontally</em> does no work against gravity, because the motion is perpendicular
    to the weight. And merely <em>holding</em> the box does no work at all — force without
    distance. Friction, meanwhile, always charges for distance: drag the box 3 m with a 50 N
    horizontal force and you pay 150 J, most of which friction promptly converts to thermal
    energy in the floor.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "holding heavy luggage is hard work, so I am doing work."
  It feels like work because your muscles keep twitching and burning chemical energy — but the
  luggage does not move, so <em>physics</em> work on the luggage is zero. A sturdy table holds
  the same suitcase all day for free. Effort is biology; work is force × distance.</p>

  <h2>Power: how fast the payment happens</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Power</strong> is energy transferred per second: <code>P = E / t</code>, measured in
    watts (1 W = 1 J/s). Energy tells you <em>how much</em>; power tells you <em>how fast</em>.
    Same staircase, two speeds: a 60 kg student climbing 3 m gains 60 × 9.8 × 3 ≈ 1,760 J.
    Sprint it in 5 s and the useful power is 1,760 ÷ 5 ≈ 350 W. Walk it gently in 15 s and
    the same 1,760 J transfers at only 117 W. Same energy, different power.
  </p>
  <p>
    Household scale: an electric kettle draws about 2,000 W and boils a litre in a couple of
    minutes; a typical car engine delivers up to 100,000 W flat out. The everyday trap:
    electricity bills are priced in <em>kilowatt-hours</em> — the energy of a 1 kW appliance
    running for an hour. Kilowatt is power; kilowatt-hour is energy. The panel below collects every formula on this page — set a mass and volume to see
    density, then a climb and a time to read off both the energy bill and the power it took.
  </p>
          
  <Widget no="2" caption="three formulas, one dial panel — density, energy of a climb, and the power it takes" widget="sliderLab" config={workshopCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> slide the mass and volume and watch the density readout
    separate materials — same number whether the object is big or small. Then slide the climb
    height and the time: the energy bill responds to height, the power bill to <em>how fast</em>
    you did it. Two different questions, two different readouts.
  </p>

  <h2>Density: why some things float</h2>
  <p>
    One measurement deserves its own section because it decides whether things float, sink or
    fly. <strong>Density</strong> is how tightly matter is packed: <code>ρ = m / V</code> — mass
    divided by volume, in kg/m³ (or g/cm³, where 1 g/cm³ = 1,000 kg/m³). Water is 1.0 g/cm³;
    air about 0.0012; lead 11.3. Density belongs to the <em>material</em>, not the object: a
    gram of lead and a tonne of lead have the same density.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example: a metal block has mass 120 g and dimensions 4 cm × 3 cm × 2.5 cm. Volume
    = 4 × 3 × 2.5 = 30 cm³, so density = 120 ÷ 30 = 4.0 g/cm³ — plausible for a titanium alloy.
    For an irregular object, immerse it: the rise in water level equals its volume (the trick
    Archimedes used to test a king's crown). For a liquid, weigh a known volume from a measuring
    cylinder and divide.
  </p>
  <p>
    Density decides floating. An object floats if its average density is below the fluid's,
    because the fluid can push up with a force equal to the weight of fluid displaced. Ice
    (0.92 g/cm³) floats on water with about 92% of it hidden below the surface — which is why
    icebergs deceive ships and why lakes freeze from the top down, insulating the fish below.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "heavy things sink." It seems reasonable — every heavy
  thing you have dropped in a bath has sunk. But it is the wrong comparison: what matters is
  density compared with the fluid, not weight alone. The correct picture: a 100,000-tonne ship
  floats happily because its average density — steel hull <em>plus</em> all the air inside — is
  below water's, while a tiny pebble sinks because its density is above water's. Contrast the
  two: the ship weighs more than the pebble by a factor of billions, yet one floats and the
  other sinks.</p>

  <h2>Pressure: force spread over area</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Pressure</strong> is force concentrated or spread: <code>p = F / A</code> — force
    divided by the area it acts over, in pascals (1 Pa = 1 N/m²). The same force can be
    devastating or harmless depending on the area. A drawing pin: your thumb supplies about
    30 N, concentrated on a tip a fraction of a millimetre across — tens of millions of pascals,
    enough to pierce wood — while the broad flat head spreads the same 30 N so your thumb feels
    nothing sharp. A 60 kg person standing on both feet presses at about 15,000 Pa; on a
    stiletto heel, millions.
  </p>
  <p>
    In fluids, pressure grows with depth because the weight of fluid above stacks up:
    <code>p = ρ·g·h</code>. Dive 10 m underwater and you add about 98,000 Pa — roughly one extra
    atmosphere — which is why your ears pop. Fluid pressure acts in all directions and transmits
    unchanged through a connected fluid: that is hydraulics. A small force on a small piston
    becomes a large force on a large piston — at the cost of pushing the small piston much
    further. Force is multiplied, work is conserved: the accounting survives once again.
  </p>

  <h2>Where civilisation's energy comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Every power station, panel and turbine on Earth is rearranging the same energy budget.
    <strong>Non-renewable</strong> resources draw down stores that took geologic ages to fill:
    fossil fuels (chemical, ancient sunlight) and uranium (nuclear). <strong>Renewables</strong>
    tap flows that keep arriving: sunlight, wind (the Sun's heating of the air), hydroelectric
    (rain lifted by evaporation), biomass, geothermal heat, tides (the Moon's pull). Each choice
    trades off energy density, reliability, cost and environmental damage.
  </p>
  <p>
    No conversion is perfect, and <strong>efficiency</strong> measures the shortfall:
    <code>efficiency = useful energy out ÷ total energy in</code>. An old incandescent bulb
    turns about 5% of its electricity into light — the rest heats the room. An LED manages above
    50%. A thermal power station converts roughly 35–40% of its fuel into electricity, dumping
    the rest as low-grade heat — a limit imposed by thermodynamics, not poor engineering.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "energy gets used up — that is why there is an energy
  crisis." It sounds reasonable because we speak of "using" energy daily. But the first law
  forbids it: after your car journey, every joule that started in the petrol still exists. What
  changed is its <em>quality</em> — it is now a spread-out warm haze of exhaust heat and road
  friction, too diffuse to push anything. The crisis is a <em>useful-energy</em> crisis, not an
  energy one.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In your own words: what does the conservation of energy claim?</li>
    <li>A 70 kg hiker climbs a 4 m staircase. How much gravitational potential energy do they gain?</li>
    <li>Why must the first hill of a roller-coaster be the tallest?</li>
    <li>Your electricity bill charges for "kilowatt-hours". Is that a quantity of energy or of power?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Energy is never created or destroyed — only transferred
    between stores or spread out. (2) GPE = m·g·h = 70 × 9.8 × 4 ≈ 2,744 J; if you squared
    something, you reached for the kinetic formula by mistake. (3) Friction leaks energy along
    the track, so later hills must be shorter — the cart only has the budget the first hill
    loaded. (4) Energy — a power times a time is an amount of energy delivered.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Energy is conserved: never created or destroyed, only transferred between stores or spread out.</li>
    <li>Kinetic energy KE = ½·m·v² (the square of speed matters); gravitational potential energy GPE = m·g·h.</li>
    <li>Work W = F·d is energy paid by a force along a distance — no distance, no work.</li>
    <li>Power P = E/t is the <em>rate</em>; a kilowatt-hour on your bill is energy, not power.</li>
    <li>Density ρ = m/V decides floating — compared with the fluid, never weight alone.</li>
    <li>Pressure p = F/A grows with depth in fluids (p = ρ·g·h) and powers hydraulics.</li>
    <li>Efficiency = useful ÷ total: the missing energy still exists, but it is no longer useful.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Energy gives you the "why" of motion; the next lessons supply the "how" — forces explaining
    <em>why the cart speeds up, slows down and turns</em>. The same accounting explains engines,
    electricity generation and the climate choices above. And in thermal physics you will meet
    the law that puts a ceiling on efficiency at all.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Where does <code>KE = ½·m·v²</code> come from? Not from thin air — from the work definition
    plus the equations of motion. Accelerate a mass m from rest with force F over distance s:
    the work done is F·s. From <code>v² = u² + 2·a·s</code> with u = 0, s = v²/(2a), so
    F·s = (m·a)·(v²/2a) = ½·m·v². The ½ is not arbitrary; it is the fingerprint of distance
    growing as the <em>square</em> of time during acceleration — which is exactly why braking
    distance scales with the square of speed. Two different roads to one formula is what makes
    physicists call a result solid.
  </p>
  <p>
    The energy idea reaches further than the syllabus suggests. Chemical energy turns out to be
    electrical potential energy between atoms; thermal energy is kinetic energy of
    sub-microscopic jiggling; mass itself is a store — Einstein's <code>E = m·c²</code> means
    1 kg of matter is worth about 9 × 10¹⁶ J, and the Sun converts roughly 4 million tonnes of
    mass to radiated energy every second. At the frontier, cosmology's biggest open problem is
    a form of energy: "dark energy", about 68% of the universe, whose nature nobody knows. The
    accounting runs from a drawing pin to the cosmos — no exception ever found.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> OpenStax, <em>College Physics</em> (Work and Kinetic Energy; Fluid Mechanics); Feynman, <em>Lectures on Physics</em>, Vol. I; Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus (2026–2028), sections 1.4, 1.7 and 1.8; IEA, Key World Energy Statistics.</p>

</TopicPage>